  <!-- Section 7: Estimation Methodology for Closed Economies -->
  <section id="estimations" class="flex flex-col gap-4 border-t border-border/40 pt-8">
    <div class="flex items-center gap-2 text-primary font-bold text-sm uppercase tracking-wider">
      <Sparkles className="size-4" />
      <span>7. Econometric Estimations for Closed & Conflict Economies</span>
    </div>
    <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold tracking-tight text-foreground">Estimating Data for Nations with Restricted Reporting</h2>

    <div class="prose dark:prose-invert max-w-none text-xs sm:text-sm text-muted-foreground leading-relaxed flex flex-col gap-3">
      <p>
        To ensure complete global coverage across all <strong>195 sovereign nations</strong>, AtlasIndex provides econometric estimates for 19 states where official statistical agencies do not publish standard consumer price indexes or regular labor surveys (such as <strong>North Korea, Eritrea, Syria, Afghanistan, Cuba, Haiti, Somalia, Yemen, and South Sudan</strong>).
      </p>
      <p>
        For these economies, data is derived using a multi-source triangulation framework:
      </p>
      <div class="grid grid-cols-1 sm:grid-cols-3 gap-3 my-2 text-xs">
        <div class="p-3 rounded-lg border border-border/60 bg-background/50">
          <strong class="text-foreground block mb-1">1. UN Humanitarian Monitoring</strong>
          <span class="text-muted-foreground">Market price surveillance and vulnerability assessments by the UN World Food Programme (WFP VAM) and UN FAO GIEWS.</span>
        </div>
        <div class="p-3 rounded-lg border border-border/60 bg-background/50">
          <strong class="text-foreground block mb-1">2. Regional PPP Parity Models</strong>
          <span class="text-muted-foreground">World Bank International Comparison Program (ICP) purchasing power parity regression benchmarks and neighboring country baskets.</span>
        </div>
        <div class="p-3 rounded-lg border border-border/60 bg-background/50">
          <strong class="text-foreground block mb-1">3. Cross-Border & Informal Data</strong>
          <span class="text-muted-foreground">For closed economies like North Korea, informal market surveys (Jangmadang market basket reports from Daily NK / Asia Press) and Bank of Korea models.</span>
        </div>
      </div>
      <p>
        All estimated economies are explicitly labeled with an <span class="text-amber-500 font-semibold">"Est." badge</span> and detailed disclaimers across all tables, charts, and comparison tools.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- Section 8: Limitations & Caveats -->
  <section id="limitations" class="flex flex-col gap-4 border-t border-border/40 pt-8">
    <div class="flex items-center gap-2 text-amber-500 font-bold text-sm uppercase tracking-wider">
      <AlertTriangle className="size-4" />
      <span>8. Known Limitations & Research Scope</span>
    </div>
    <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold tracking-tight text-foreground">Methodological Constraints</h2>

    <div class="space-y-3 text-xs sm:text-sm text-muted-foreground leading-relaxed">
      <div class="rounded-xl border border-border/80 bg-card/60 p-4">
        <h4 class="font-bold text-foreground mb-1">1. Regional & Urban/Rural Variance</h4>
        <p>
          Prices represent national weighted averages. Food prices in capital cities (e.g. New York, London, Tokyo, São Paulo) may be 10–25% higher than in agricultural regions, although metropolitan wages are also typically higher.
        </p>
      </div>

      <div class="rounded-xl border border-border/80 bg-card/60 p-4">
        <h4 class="font-bold text-foreground mb-1">2. Net vs Gross Wage Adjustments</h4>
        <p>
          Where available, take-home (net) median earnings are utilized. For countries where statistical agencies only publish gross earnings, standard single-earner tax wedges (OECD Taxing Wages model) are applied to arrive at disposable purchasing power.
        </p>
      </div>

      <div class="rounded-xl border border-border/80 bg-card/60 p-4">
        <h4 class="font-bold text-foreground mb-1">3. Informal Sector Dynamics</h4>
        <p>
          In emerging economies with large informal sectors (e.g., India, Nigeria, Indonesia), official survey median wages may underestimate informal cash labor or agricultural subsistence production.
        </p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- Section 9: FAQ Accordion -->
  <section id="faq" class="flex flex-col gap-4 border-t border-border/40 pt-8">
    <div class="flex items-center gap-2 text-primary font-bold text-sm uppercase tracking-wider">
      <HelpCircle className="size-4" />
      <span>9. Frequently Asked Questions</span>
    </div>
    <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold tracking-tight text-foreground">Frequently Asked Questions</h2>

    <div class="space-y-3 text-xs sm:text-sm">
      <Card className="border-border/80 bg-card/60">
        <CardHeader className="pb-2">
          <CardTitle className="text-sm font-bold">Why not use the Mean Average Wage?</CardTitle>
        </CardHeader>
        <CardContent className="text-muted-foreground text-xs leading-relaxed">
          Mean average wages are heavily skewed upwards by ultra-high earners (CEOs, tech executives, financiers). Median wage reflects the 50th percentile—exactly where the middle worker sits in society.
        </CardContent>
      </Card>

      <Card className="border-border/80 bg-card/60">
        <CardHeader className="pb-2">
          <CardTitle className="text-sm font-bold">What is the difference between APPI and PPP (Purchasing Power Parity)?</CardTitle>
        </CardHeader>
        <CardContent className="text-muted-foreground text-xs leading-relaxed">
          Standard World Bank PPP creates an artificial "international dollar" based on a massive hypothetical basket including machinery and government services. APPI grounds purchasing power in <strong>actual median hours of labor</strong> for tangible essentials (food, rent, car, healthcare), making it immediately intuitive and reflective of real citizen purchasing power.
        </CardContent>
      </Card>

      <Card className="border-border/80 bg-card/60">
        <CardHeader className="pb-2">
          <CardTitle className="text-sm font-bold">How often is the dataset refreshed?</CardTitle>
        </CardHeader>
        <CardContent className="text-muted-foreground text-xs leading-relaxed">
          AtlasIndex is audited bi-annually against newly released quarterly labor surveys (BLS, Eurostat, IBGE, ONS) and inflation reports. The current dataset reflects the 2025 release.
        </CardContent>
      </Card>

      <Card className="border-border/80 bg-card/60">
        <CardHeader className="pb-2">
          <CardTitle className="text-sm font-bold">Can I use this data for academic research or publications?</CardTitle>
        </CardHeader>
        <CardContent className="text-muted-foreground text-xs leading-relaxed">
          Yes! All AtlasIndex data is open and licensed under Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International (CC BY 4.0). You can export raw CSV and JSON datasets directly from the Rankings page.
        </CardContent>
      </Card>
    </div>
  </section>

